nodecode-chatto is a rewrite of jrimmer's jrimmer/hermes-chatto-plugin:

  https://github.com/jrimmer/hermes-chatto-plugin
  commit aab0a20635cb578253a8014bc8d93e1b32f020fc
  MIT, Copyright (c) Nous Research (LICENSE; the repository carries no
  LICENSE file, and its plugin.yaml names that author and licence)

What was rewritten: the lane as a whole -- logging in to a Chatto server with
an account and password, reading the rooms a bot is in, reading room events,
posting answers with the ask's thread, the mention rule (@login in a room, any
message in a direct message) and the poll that keeps it all going. The
ConnectRPC paths and their JSON bodies are the original's; the WebSocket
realtime transport, the binary protobuf codec that fed it, and the Hermes
gateway own plumbing around them are not carried over.

What was left out and why: the original listens on Chatto's realtime
WebSocket, decoding binary protobuf frames by hand; this lane polls the same
server's ConnectRPC methods every few seconds instead, so it needs no codec
and no long-lived socket, at the cost of a reply landing a few seconds later
than a message arrives. Message editing is not modelled either -- Chatto's
edit call is not carried -- so a status card that would be edited arrives as
a fresh line in the same thread. File uploads, typing indicators beyond one
beat, reactions and the Hermes-specific cron delivery, presence broadcasting
and heartbeat file are left to the host cell that draws the answer.
